sum='Kezia is afraid of her father – a big man with a loud voice who gives orders, checks on her and is strict. She feels a glad sense of relief when the noise of his carriage grows fainter as he leaves for the office. He has breakfast, reads the newspaper and asks for his shoes before leaving; in the evening he comes home, has tea and lies on the sofa with a handkerchief on his face, snoring; on Sundays he stretches out on the sofa and wants quiet. Kezia stutters when she speaks to him. Her grandmother asks her to make a pin-cushion of yellow silk as a birthday gift for him; Kezia stuffs it with the papers she finds on his table – his great speech for the Port Authority. Father is angry and punishes her by slapping her hands with a ruler. She cannot understand why God made fathers. Then she watches the Macdonalds next door: Mr. Macdonald plays with his children and laughs – he is a very different father. One night, when Mother is in hospital and Grannie has gone with her, Kezia has a nightmare about a butcher. Father comforts her, takes her into his own bed and she sleeps beside him. Hearing him say that he is tired and seeing him fall asleep, she realises that he has to work hard and is too tired to play; she feels a funny feeling and says “Oh, what a big heart you’ve got, Father dear.” Poem “Rain on the Roof”: on a rainy night the poet lies in his cottage bed listening to the patter of rain; every tinkle on the roof brings dreams and memories – especially of his mother, who used to tuck him and his brothers (“darling dreamers”) in bed with loving looks; she is no longer alive, but the rain brings back her memory.',
